<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- SPDX-License-Identifier: CC-BY-4.0 -->
<TEI xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0" xmlns:rng="http://relaxng.org/ns/structure/1.0"
  xmlns:sch="http://purl.oclc.org/dsdl/schematron" xml:lang="en">
  <teiHeader>
    <fileDesc>
      <titleStmt>
        <title>The Solemne TEI schema</title>
        <author>Gleb Schmidt</author>
        <author>Friederike Voit</author>
        <author>Sven Meeder</author>
      </titleStmt>
      <editionStmt>
        <edition n="0.1.0">Version 0.1.0</edition>
      </editionStmt>
      <publicationStmt>
        <publisher>SOLEMNE: The Social Life of Early Medieval Normative Texts, Radboud University</publisher>
        <availability>
          <licence target="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">This ODD, the guide: CC BY 4.0.</licence>
          <licence target="https://opensource.org/license/mit">The schema generated from it: MIT. It contains
            material of TEI P5 (the TEI Consortium: CC BY 3.0 and BSD 2-Clause) and of the Bodleian Libraries'
            consolidated TEI schema (BSD 2-Clause), and its compiled Schematron the code of SchXslt2 (David Maus,
            MIT): their notices are at the top of each generated file.</licence>
        </availability>
      </publicationStmt>
      <sourceDesc>
        <p>Written for the project, on TEI P5 and the Bodleian's consolidated schema for manuscript
          descriptions.</p>
      </sourceDesc>
    </fileDesc>
  </teiHeader>
  <text>
    <body>
      <head>The Solemne TEI schema</head>
      <p>The schema of the TEI files the application imports and exports: early medieval canon law texts, their
        structure, what is marked in them, and the records they refer to. It is generated into RelaxNG, which the
        import requires, and Schematron, whose findings the import reports and the exports must pass.</p>

      <div>
        <head>The text's structure</head>
        <p>A document is a tree of divisions. A division holds headings, then either paragraphs or divisions,
          never both; headings may stand where the encoding puts them, usually first. Page breaks, notes and gaps
          may stand between them. The body itself, the document's root, follows the same rule.</p>
        <p>Every division and heading has a type, from a closed list, so that a misspelled type fails. A numbered
          division carries its number as a value in <att>n</att> (49 for a heading XLVIIII; 11a for a chapter
          after 11), and its number heading keeps the number as written. A unit is a subdivision without a
          heading of its own, written only where units stand side by side: a division whose text is one unit
          holds its paragraphs itself.</p>
        <p>The paragraphs of a division that holds no divisions are one unit of reception. Where the encoders
          didn't mark the units, the header says so once, in <gi>segmentation</gi> with the type
          <val>inferred-units</val>.</p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <head>What is marked in the text</head>
        <p>Inside the body, only the elements of the annotation layers appear: names of persons, places and
          events; works named; statements with their reference and quotation; formulas; dates; notes of three
          types (sources, parallels, editorial remarks); scribal changes; what is unclear or missing; page and
          line breaks; other languages; and editorial corrections.</p>
        <p>A quotation and a note point to their sources in <att>source</att>, in one of these forms: a document's
          code (<code>C.NIC-0325</code>), alone or with a citation after an underscore
          (<code>C.NIC-0325_3</code>, <code>COLL.Dach.ms-Brn-Burg-425_2.49</code>); a Bible reference, by one
          code per book, chapter and verse (<code>MAT_18_19</code>, <code>PHP_3_17-20</code>); a work by its
          Gryson siglum, with the place cited (<code>IS_ety_1.2.3</code>); or an SCTA resource. A loose reference
          starts with <code>cf_</code>. Names point to their records by placeholders (<code>#new_person_…</code>)
          until the import resolves them, then by permalinks.</p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <head>The header</head>
        <p>The title statement opens with a formal title holding exactly three identifiers: the document's code,
          its category and its type. Further titles are aliases or other titles. Dates are given as one point
          (<att>when</att>) or as a span with both bounds. Persons, places and events listed in the standOff
          either point to a record or describe one.</p>
      </div>

      <div>
        <head>Exports</head>
        <p>Exports add what inline markup can't hold: the citation structure (<gi>citeStructure</gi>);
          annotations in the standOff (<gi>listAnnotation</gi>); relations (<gi>listRelation</gi>), each an
          observation or a judgement; text reuse as link groups (<gi>linkGrp</gi>); the revision history; and
          the records, as manuscript descriptions after the Bodleian's rules, bibliographic records and lists of
          persons, places, events and organizations.</p>
      </div>

      <schemaSpec ident="solemne" start="TEI" docLang="en" targetLang="en">

        <!-- The modules, and the elements taken from each. -->
        <moduleRef key="tei"/>
        <moduleRef key="core"
          include="abbr add addrLine address analytic author bibl biblScope biblStruct choice cit citedRange corr
            date del desc editor email expan foreign gap graphic head imprint item label lb list listBibl measure
            monogr name note num orig p pb postCode ptr publisher pubPlace q quote ref reg resp respStmt series sic
            street term textLang title unclear"/>
        <moduleRef key="header"
          include="abstract authority availability catDesc category change citeData citeStructure classDecl
            correction creation distributor edition editionStmt editorialDecl encodingDesc extent fileDesc funder
            handNote idno interpretation keywords langUsage language licence listChange normalization notesStmt
            principal profileDesc projectDesc publicationStmt punctuation quotation refsDecl revisionDesc
            samplingDecl scriptNote segmentation seriesStmt sourceDesc sponsor stdVals taxonomy teiHeader
            textClass titleStmt typeNote"/>
        <moduleRef key="textstructure" include="TEI body div text"/>
        <moduleRef key="namesdates"
          include="addName affiliation birth bloc country death district event eventName floruit forename genName
            geo geogName listEvent listOrg listPerson listPlace listRelation location nameLink occupation org
            orgName persName person place placeName region relation roleName settlement surname"/>
        <!-- Manuscript descriptions follow the Bodleian's consolidated schema: the build compiles its ODD and puts
             it next to this one under this name. -->
        <moduleRef key="msdescription" source="msdesc.compiled.odd"/>
        <moduleRef key="linking" include="annotation link linkGrp listAnnotation seg standOff"/>
        <moduleRef key="transcr" include="subst"/>
        <moduleRef key="textcrit" include="listWit witness"/>

        <!-- The structure of the text. -->
        <macroSpec ident="solemne.structure" type="pe" mode="add">
          <desc>What a division or the body holds: headings with either paragraphs or divisions, never both, and
            page breaks, notes and gaps between them.</desc>
          <content>
            <alternate>
              <alternate minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
                <elementRef key="head"/>
                <macroRef key="solemne.between"/>
                <elementRef key="p"/>
              </alternate>
              <alternate minOccurs="0" maxOccurs="unbounded">
                <elementRef key="head"/>
                <macroRef key="solemne.between"/>
                <elementRef key="div"/>
              </alternate>
            </alternate>
          </content>
        </macroSpec>
        <macroSpec ident="solemne.between" type="pe" mode="add">
          <desc>What may stand between headings, paragraphs and divisions.</desc>
          <content>
            <alternate>
              <elementRef key="pb"/>
              <elementRef key="note"/>
              <elementRef key="gap"/>
            </alternate>
          </content>
        </macroSpec>

        <elementSpec ident="body" module="textstructure" mode="change">
          <content>
            <macroRef key="solemne.structure"/>
          </content>
          <constraintSpec ident="text-elements" scheme="schematron">
            <desc>Inside the text, only the annotation layers' elements.</desc>
            <constraint>
              <sch:rule context="tei:body//tei:*">
                <sch:assert id="text-elements" test="local-name() = ('div', 'head', 'p', 'note', 'pb', 'lb', 'gap',
                  'persName', 'placeName', 'eventName', 'bibl', 'cit', 'quote', 'ref', 'seg', 'date', 'subst',
                  'add', 'del', 'unclear', 'foreign', 'choice', 'corr', 'sic')">&lt;<sch:name/>&gt; isn't one of
                  the text's elements.</sch:assert>
              </sch:rule>
            </constraint>
          </constraintSpec>
          <constraintSpec ident="text-note-type" scheme="schematron">
            <desc>In the text, a note's type is its layer.</desc>
            <constraint>
              <sch:rule context="tei:body//tei:note">
                <sch:assert id="text-note-type" test="@type = ('source', 'parallel', 'editorial')">A note in the
                  text is typed source, parallel or editorial.</sch:assert>
              </sch:rule>
            </constraint>
          </constraintSpec>
          <constraintSpec ident="note-has-content" scheme="schematron">
            <desc>A note holds words, or its codes in source.</desc>
            <constraint>
              <sch:rule context="tei:note">
                <sch:assert id="note-has-content" test="normalize-space(.) != '' or @source or *">A note holds
                  words, or its codes in @source.</sch:assert>
              </sch:rule>
            </constraint>
          </constraintSpec>
          <constraintSpec ident="source-note-codes" scheme="schematron">
            <desc>A source note's codes are in source, not in its words.</desc>
            <constraint>
              <sch:rule context="tei:body//tei:note[@type = 'source']">
                <sch:report id="source-note-codes" role="warning" test="some $w in tokenize(normalize-space(.), '[\s;,]+')
                  satisfies matches($w, '^(cf_)?((C|D|COLL|P|EP|H|EX|W)\.[A-Za-z0-9.\-]+(_[A-Za-z0-9.\-]+)?|[1-4]?[A-Z]{2,4}_[0-9]+(_[0-9]+)?(-[0-9]+)?)$')">A
                  source note's words hold a code (<sch:value-of select="normalize-space(.)"/>): it belongs in
                  @source.</sch:report>
              </sch:rule>
            </constraint>
          </constraintSpec>
        </elementSpec>

        <elementSpec ident="div" module="textstructure" mode="change">
          <content>
            <macroRef key="solemne.structure"/>
          </content>
          <constraintSpec ident="unit-alone" scheme="schematron">
            <desc>Units are written only where they stand side by side.</desc>
            <constraint>
              <sch:rule context="tei:div[@type = 'unit']">
                <sch:assert id="unit-alone" test="count(../tei:div) gt 1">A unit stands alone in its division: its
                  paragraphs belong to the division itself.</sch:assert>
                <sch:assert id="unit-holds-text" test="not(tei:div) and not(tei:head)">A unit holds paragraphs
                  only, without headings or divisions.</sch:assert>
              </sch:rule>
            </constraint>
          </constraintSpec>
          <constraintSpec ident="register-item-in-register" scheme="schematron">
            <desc>A register's items stand in a register.</desc>
            <constraint>
              <sch:rule context="tei:div[@type = 'registerItem']">
                <sch:assert id="register-item-in-register" test="parent::tei:div[@type = 'register']">A register
                  item stands in a register.</sch:assert>
              </sch:rule>
            </constraint>
          </constraintSpec>
          <constraintSpec ident="number-heading-n" scheme="schematron">
            <desc>A division with a number heading carries the number as a value.</desc>
            <constraint>
              <sch:rule context="tei:div[tei:head[@type = 'number']]">
                <sch:assert id="number-heading-n" test="@n">A division with a number heading
                  (<sch:value-of select="normalize-space(tei:head[@type = 'number'][1])"/>) has @n.</sch:assert>
              </sch:rule>
            </constraint>
          </constraintSpec>
          <attList>
            <attDef ident="type" mode="change" usage="req">
              <valList type="closed" mode="replace">
                <valItem ident="section">
                  <desc>a book, a part, or one text inside a document (a council in a collection)</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="chapter">
                  <desc>a numbered or titled unit; chapters may hold chapters</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="unit">
                  <desc>a subdivision without a heading of its own, where several stand side by side: a norm and its
                    authority statements, one signatory among the subscriptions</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="preface"/>
                <valItem ident="closer">
                  <desc>a closing rubric, or a letter's farewell and date</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="subscriptions">
                  <desc>the signatories</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="register">
                  <desc>the list of chapter titles</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="registerItem">
                  <desc>one entry of a register</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="part">
                  <desc>a part of Gratian's Decretum</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="distinctio">
                  <desc>a distinction of Gratian's Decretum</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="causa">
                  <desc>a causa of Gratian's Decretum</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="quaestio">
                  <desc>a question of a causa</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="capitulum">
                  <desc>a canon of Gratian's Decretum</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="dictum">
                  <desc>Gratian's own words between the canons</desc>
                </valItem>
              </valList>
            </attDef>
            <attDef ident="n" mode="change">
              <desc>the division's number, as a value: 49 for a heading XLVIIII; a letter after it for a chapter
                after a numbered one, or for a number repeated</desc>
              <datatype>
                <dataRef name="token" restriction="[0-9]+[a-z]?"/>
              </datatype>
            </attDef>
            <attDef ident="subtype" mode="delete"/>
          </attList>
        </elementSpec>

        <elementSpec ident="head" module="core" mode="change">
          <attList>
            <attDef ident="type" mode="change" usage="req">
              <valList type="closed" mode="replace">
                <valItem ident="number">
                  <desc>the division's number as written ("Cap. II.")</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="rubric">
                  <desc>what the text is about ("De subintroductis mulieribus")</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="inscription">
                  <desc>where the text comes from ("Ex concilio Nicaeno"), even when it goes on with its
                    subject</desc>
                </valItem>
              </valList>
            </attDef>
            <attDef ident="subtype" mode="delete"/>
          </attList>
        </elementSpec>

        <elementSpec ident="note" module="core" mode="change">
          <attList>
            <attDef ident="type" mode="change">
              <valList type="closed" mode="replace">
                <valItem ident="source">
                  <desc>in the text: the source a passage comes from, its codes in source</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="parallel">
                  <desc>in the text: a parallel elsewhere ("Cf. concil. Mogunt. 847 c. 3")</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="editorial">
                  <desc>in the text: an editor's remark</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="links">
                  <desc>in a record: the links to the record in other catalogues</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="authorities">
                  <desc>in a record: the authority files that describe it</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="extra">
                  <desc>in a bibliographic record: what fits nowhere else</desc>
                </valItem>
              </valList>
            </attDef>
            <attDef ident="source" mode="change">
              <desc>the sources the note points to: see the pointers' forms</desc>
              <datatype minOccurs="1" maxOccurs="unbounded">
                <macroRef key="solemne.pointer"/>
              </datatype>
            </attDef>
            <attDef ident="subtype" mode="delete"/>
          </attList>
        </elementSpec>

        <elementSpec ident="quote" module="core" mode="change">
          <attList>
            <attDef ident="source" mode="change">
              <desc>the source quoted: see the pointers' forms</desc>
              <datatype minOccurs="1" maxOccurs="unbounded">
                <macroRef key="solemne.pointer"/>
              </datatype>
            </attDef>
            <attDef ident="type" mode="delete"/>
            <attDef ident="subtype" mode="delete"/>
          </attList>
        </elementSpec>

        <elementSpec ident="seg" module="linking" mode="change">
          <attList>
            <attDef ident="type" mode="change" usage="req">
              <valList type="closed" mode="replace">
                <valItem ident="formula">
                  <desc>a formula introducing an authority ("ut lex dicit")</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="corr">
                  <desc>a scribe's correction</desc>
                </valItem>
              </valList>
            </attDef>
            <attDef ident="subtype" mode="delete"/>
          </attList>
        </elementSpec>

        <elementSpec ident="ref" module="core" mode="change">
          <attList>
            <attDef ident="type" mode="change">
              <valList type="closed" mode="replace">
                <valItem ident="from_paratext">
                  <desc>a statement's reference whose claim sits in a heading</desc>
                </valItem>
              </valList>
            </attDef>
          </attList>
        </elementSpec>

        <elementSpec ident="pb" module="core" mode="change">
          <attList>
            <attDef ident="n" mode="change" usage="req">
              <desc>the page, folio or column: 255, 120ra, 0187A</desc>
            </attDef>
          </attList>
        </elementSpec>

        <!-- Names point to their records: a placeholder until the import resolves it, then a permalink. -->
        <macroSpec ident="solemne.recordPointer" type="dt" mode="add">
          <desc>A pointer to a record: a placeholder (#new_person_…), an identifier in the file, or a
            permalink.</desc>
          <content>
            <dataRef name="token" restriction="#new_[a-z]+_\S+|#[A-Za-z_][A-Za-z0-9_.\-]*|https?://\S+"/>
          </content>
        </macroSpec>
        <elementSpec ident="persName" module="namesdates" mode="change">
          <attList>
            <attDef ident="ref" mode="change">
              <datatype minOccurs="1" maxOccurs="unbounded">
                <macroRef key="solemne.recordPointer"/>
              </datatype>
            </attDef>
          </attList>
        </elementSpec>
        <elementSpec ident="placeName" module="namesdates" mode="change">
          <attList>
            <attDef ident="ref" mode="change">
              <datatype minOccurs="1" maxOccurs="unbounded">
                <macroRef key="solemne.recordPointer"/>
              </datatype>
            </attDef>
          </attList>
        </elementSpec>
        <elementSpec ident="eventName" module="namesdates" mode="change">
          <attList>
            <attDef ident="ref" mode="change">
              <datatype minOccurs="1" maxOccurs="unbounded">
                <macroRef key="solemne.recordPointer"/>
              </datatype>
            </attDef>
          </attList>
        </elementSpec>
        <elementSpec ident="bibl" module="core" mode="change">
          <attList>
            <attDef ident="ref" mode="change">
              <datatype minOccurs="1" maxOccurs="unbounded">
                <macroRef key="solemne.recordPointer"/>
              </datatype>
            </attDef>
          </attList>
        </elementSpec>

        <!-- The pointers' forms, for quote/@source and note/@source. -->
        <macroSpec ident="solemne.pointer" type="dt" mode="add">
          <desc>A pointer to a source: a document's code, alone or with a citation after an underscore
            (C.NIC-0325_3); a Bible reference by book, chapter and verse (MAT_18_19, PHP_3_17-20); a Gryson siglum
            and the place cited (IS_ety_1.2.3); each may start with cf_ for a loose reference; or an SCTA
            resource. A code holds letters, digits, dots and hyphens; an underscore only separates a code from its
            citation.</desc>
          <content>
            <dataRef name="token"
              restriction="(cf_)?((C|D|COLL|P|EP|H|EX|W)\.[A-Za-z0-9]+([.\-][A-Za-z0-9]+)*(_[A-Za-z0-9]+([.\-][A-Za-z0-9]+)*)?|(GEN|EXO|LEV|NUM|DEU|JOS|JDG|RUT|1SA|2SA|1KI|2KI|1CH|2CH|EZR|NEH|TOB|JDT|EST|1MA|2MA|JOB|PSA|PRO|ECC|SOS|WIS|SIR|ISA|JER|LAM|BAR|EZE|DAN|HOS|JOE|AMO|OBA|JON|MIC|NAH|HAB|ZEP|HAG|ZEC|MAL|MAT|MAR|LUK|JOH|ACT|ROM|1CO|2CO|GAL|EPH|PHP|COL|1TH|2TH|1TI|2TI|TIT|PHM|HEB|JAM|1PE|2PE|1JO|2JO|3JO|JUD|REV)_[0-9]+(-[0-9]+)?(_[0-9]+(-[0-9]+)?)?|[A-Z][A-Za-z]*(-[A-Z][A-Za-z]*)*_[A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9]*(\.[A-Za-z0-9]+)*(_[A-Za-z0-9]+([.\-][A-Za-z0-9]+)*)?)|https?://scta\.info/resource/\S+"/>
          </content>
        </macroSpec>

        <!-- The units of reception, where the encoders didn't mark them. -->
        <elementSpec ident="segmentation" module="header" mode="change">
          <classes mode="change">
            <memberOf key="att.typed"/>
          </classes>
          <attList>
            <attDef ident="type" mode="change">
              <valList type="closed" mode="replace">
                <valItem ident="inferred-units">
                  <desc>the units of reception aren't marked: each division that holds paragraphs is
                    one</desc>
                </valItem>
              </valList>
            </attDef>
            <attDef ident="subtype" mode="delete"/>
          </attList>
        </elementSpec>

        <!-- The header. -->
        <elementSpec ident="titleStmt" module="header" mode="change">
          <constraintSpec ident="title-statement" scheme="schematron">
            <desc>The title statement opens with the formal title; the other titles are aliases or other
              titles.</desc>
            <constraint>
              <sch:rule context="tei:fileDesc/tei:titleStmt">
                <sch:assert id="title-first-formal" test="tei:title[1][@type = 'formal']">The title statement
                  opens with a title typed formal.</sch:assert>
                <sch:assert id="title-one-formal" test="count(tei:title[@type = 'formal']) = 1">The title
                  statement has one formal title.</sch:assert>
              </sch:rule>
              <sch:rule context="tei:fileDesc/tei:titleStmt/tei:title">
                <sch:assert id="title-type" test="@type = ('formal', 'alias', 'other')">A title in the title
                  statement is typed formal, alias or other.</sch:assert>
                <sch:assert id="title-alias-words" test="not(@type = 'alias') or normalize-space(.) != ''">An
                  alias isn't empty.</sch:assert>
                <sch:assert id="title-other-source" role="warning" test="not(@type = 'other') or @source">A title
                  typed other names its source in @source.</sch:assert>
              </sch:rule>
            </constraint>
          </constraintSpec>
        </elementSpec>

        <elementSpec ident="idno" module="header" mode="change">
          <constraintSpec ident="formal-title" scheme="schematron">
            <desc>The formal title holds the document's code, category and type, once each.</desc>
            <constraint>
              <sch:rule context="tei:fileDesc/tei:titleStmt/tei:title[@type = 'formal']">
                <sch:assert id="formal-title-idnos" test="count(tei:idno) = 3 and count(tei:idno[@type =
                  'collCode']) = 1 and count(tei:idno[@type = 'collCat']) = 1 and count(tei:idno[@type =
                  'collType']) = 1">The formal title holds three identifiers: one each typed collCode, collCat and
                  collType.</sch:assert>
              </sch:rule>
              <sch:rule context="tei:fileDesc/tei:titleStmt/tei:title/tei:idno">
                <sch:assert id="idno-in-formal-title" test="parent::tei:title[@type = 'formal']">An identifier in
                  the title statement stands in the formal title.</sch:assert>
                <sch:assert id="idno-type" test="@type = ('collCode', 'collCat', 'collType')">An identifier in the
                  formal title is typed collCode, collCat or collType.</sch:assert>
              </sch:rule>
              <sch:rule context="tei:fileDesc/tei:titleStmt/tei:title/tei:idno[@type = 'collCode']">
                <sch:assert id="code-format" test="matches(normalize-space(.),
                  '^(C|D|COLL|P|EP|H|EX)\.[A-Za-z0-9]+([.\-][A-Za-z0-9]+)*$')">The document's code
                  (<sch:value-of select="normalize-space(.)"/>) begins with its category and a dot, and holds only
                  letters, digits, dots and hyphens.</sch:assert>
                <sch:assert id="code-matches-file-name" role="warning" test="not(matches(base-uri(.),
                  '/(C|D|COLL|P|EP|H|EX)\.[^/]*\.xml$')) or ends-with(base-uri(.), concat('/',
                  encode-for-uri(normalize-space(.)), '.xml'))">A file named by a code is named by its document's
                  code (<sch:value-of select="normalize-space(.)"/>).</sch:assert>
              </sch:rule>
              <sch:rule context="tei:fileDesc/tei:titleStmt/tei:title/tei:idno[@type = 'collCat']">
                <sch:assert id="category-value" test="normalize-space(.) = ('C', 'D', 'EP', 'P', 'H', 'EX',
                  'CHRCOLL', 'SYSCOLL', 'UNK')">The document's category (<sch:value-of select="normalize-space(.)"/>)
                  is one of C, D, EP, P, H, EX, CHRCOLL, SYSCOLL, UNK.</sch:assert>
              </sch:rule>
              <sch:rule context="tei:fileDesc/tei:titleStmt/tei:title/tei:idno[@type = 'collType']">
                <sch:assert id="document-type-value" test="normalize-space(.) = ('edition', 'manuscript',
                  'reconstruction', 'hypothesis', 'placeholder')">The document's type (<sch:value-of
                  select="normalize-space(.)"/>) is one of edition, manuscript, reconstruction, hypothesis,
                  placeholder.</sch:assert>
              </sch:rule>
            </constraint>
          </constraintSpec>
        </elementSpec>

        <elementSpec ident="teiHeader" module="header" mode="change">
          <constraintSpec ident="dates" scheme="schematron">
            <desc>A date is one point, or a span with both its bounds.</desc>
            <constraint>
              <sch:rule context="tei:*[@notBefore or @notAfter][not(self::tei:locus or self::tei:biblScope or
                self::tei:citedRange)]">
                <sch:assert id="date-span-bounds" role="warning" test="@notBefore and @notAfter">A span gives both
                  its bounds, @notBefore and @notAfter.</sch:assert>
              </sch:rule>
              <sch:rule context="tei:*[@from or @to][not(self::tei:locus or self::tei:biblScope or
                self::tei:citedRange)]">
                <sch:assert id="date-from-to" role="warning" test="@from and @to">A span gives both its ends,
                  @from and @to.</sch:assert>
              </sch:rule>
            </constraint>
          </constraintSpec>
        </elementSpec>

        <!-- The records in the standOff. -->
        <elementSpec ident="event" module="namesdates" mode="change">
          <constraintSpec ident="event-content" scheme="schematron">
            <desc>An event points to a record, or describes one.</desc>
            <constraint>
              <sch:rule context="tei:event">
                <sch:assert id="event-described" test="@ref or tei:eventName or tei:p">An event points to a
                  record (@ref) or describes one, with eventName or p.</sch:assert>
              </sch:rule>
              <sch:rule context="tei:event/tei:desc | tei:event/tei:p">
                <sch:assert id="event-words" test="normalize-space(.) != ''">An event's description isn't
                  empty.</sch:assert>
              </sch:rule>
            </constraint>
          </constraintSpec>
        </elementSpec>

        <elementSpec ident="person" module="namesdates" mode="change">
          <constraintSpec ident="standoff-person" scheme="schematron">
            <desc>A person listed in the standOff is a record or a placeholder for one, with a main name.</desc>
            <constraint>
              <sch:rule context="tei:standOff//tei:listPerson/tei:person">
                <sch:assert id="person-id" test="matches(@xml:id, '^(PERS\.\d+|solemne_person_\d+|new_person_\S+)$')">A
                  person in the standOff has an @xml:id: a record's (PERS. and a number, or solemne_person_ and a
                  number), or a placeholder's (new_person_ and a name).</sch:assert>
                <sch:assert id="person-main-name" test="count(tei:persName[@type = 'main']) = 1 or
                  not(starts-with(@xml:id, 'new_person_'))">A new person has one main name (persName typed
                  main).</sch:assert>
              </sch:rule>
              <sch:rule context="tei:standOff//tei:note[@type = 'links']">
                <sch:assert id="links-list" test="tei:list[@type = 'links'] and not(text()[normalize-space()])">A
                  note of links holds a list of links, and no words of its own.</sch:assert>
              </sch:rule>
              <sch:rule context="tei:standOff//tei:list[@type = 'links']/tei:item">
                <sch:assert id="link-target" test="tei:ref[@target][normalize-space(.) != '']">A link has a
                  @target and words.</sch:assert>
              </sch:rule>
            </constraint>
          </constraintSpec>
        </elementSpec>

        <elementSpec ident="floruit" module="namesdates" mode="change">
          <constraintSpec ident="floruit-dates" scheme="schematron">
            <desc>A floruit gives its dates.</desc>
            <constraint>
              <sch:rule context="tei:floruit">
                <sch:assert id="floruit-dates" test="@when or @notBefore or @notAfter or @from or @to or
                  @period">A floruit gives its dates in its attributes.</sch:assert>
              </sch:rule>
            </constraint>
          </constraintSpec>
        </elementSpec>

        <!-- Exports: relations are observations or judgements. -->
        <elementSpec ident="relation" module="namesdates" mode="change">
          <attList>
            <attDef ident="name" mode="change" usage="req"/>
            <attDef ident="type" mode="change">
              <valList type="closed" mode="replace">
                <valItem ident="observation">
                  <desc>what a witness shows</desc>
                </valItem>
                <valItem ident="judgement">
                  <desc>what holds of a version or a work, with its method</desc>
                </valItem>
              </valList>
            </attDef>
          </attList>
        </elementSpec>

      </schemaSpec>
    </body>
  </text>
</TEI>
